**Recommendation: Postgres triggers, not application-level logging.** App-level logging (e.g., `db.write()` also inserts an audit row) is simpler to write but silently breaks the moment someone adds a new write path that doesn't go through it, or a raw migration touches a row. A trigger on each audited table fires no matter how the row changed, so compliance gets a real guarantee instead of "as long as everyone remembers."

The one gap triggers don't solve on their own: they can't see `req.user`, only the SQL transaction. Close it by having `write()` set a session variable right after `BEGIN`:

```js
async function write(text, params, actorId) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SET LOCAL app.actor_id = $1", [actorId]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } ...
}
```

